WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:07.250
When the Social Security Act was passed in
1935, retirement officially began at 65.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.430
And the life expectancy at the time was 58.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:15.970
So from the very outset, “retirement”
wasn’t exactly considered a universal experience.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:21.179
But over the last century as life expectancies
have climbed, the concept of retirement has

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:24.409
become synonymous with the final chapter in
a person’s life.

00:00:24.409 --> 00:00:30.040
Then, the book “Your Money or Your Life”
came out in the 90’s and introduced a radical concept

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:35.480
The author, Vicki Robin, proposed that by
living with extreme frugality for a few years,

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.960
younger people could essentially become “retired”
long before old age.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.530
She claimed to have achieved financial independence…
in her 20’s!

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.219
Today, the phenomenon of financial independence
at a young age goes by the acronym “FIRE”.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:51.730
It stands for “Financial Independence; Retire
Early”.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.890
And it’s no fringe movement - FIRE has been
covered by the New York Times, Market Watch,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:56.890
and Forbes.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.640
And it’s got more and more millenials wondering
“could I quit my day-job too?”

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.780
This isn’t about dropping out of society
or living in a cave… necessarily.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.090
FIRE practitioners work extremely hard while
living far below their means for years to

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.270
amass enough savings to leave the workforce.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.530
And it doesn’t mean you’ll spend your
newfound freedom just hanging out in bowling

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.710
alleys like Jeff Lebowski.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:34.280
Many people who manage to retire early continue
to work--but only on projects they’re passionate about.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.380
But the question remains… is it possible
to achieve through savings alone?

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:46.100
Peter Adeney, aka “Mr. Money Mustache”,
might be considered the modern FIRE movement’s

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.110
founding father.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.810
Adeney was working as a software engineer
while living dramatically below his means

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.180
during his 20’s.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.840
He took his savings and paid off debt and
invested it it in stock-index funds.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.540
By 2005 and in his early-30’s, Adeney and
his wife had amassed around $600,000 and a

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.020
paid-for home.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.580
He calculated he had enough to leave the work-force-permanently.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.470
Adeney suggests that Early-Retirement is possible
through three fundamental concepts: Frugality,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.240
Investing, and the “4% Rule” of withdrawals.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.280
Let’s face it - unless you luck into a large
windfall of cash, you’ll have to save up

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.420
a serious nest egg to retire.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.660
And the simplest way to do that is to slash
your lifestyle.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:34.170
Normally, financial advisors suggest a 10-15%
savings rate to retire at a normal age of

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.700
65 or so.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.340
Want to retire ahead of schedule?

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.340
Then you’ll have to level that up.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.550
Most early-retirees adopt a 50% to 75% savings
rate… or more!

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.680
It’s not uncommon for them to cut restaurants
&amp; bars, buy cheap cars, bike to work, make

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.740
do with a smaller house, and avoid luxuries
like gyms, fancy vacations, and expensive hobbies.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.540
Simply stashing cash into a bank account is
a good start.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.680
But the FIRE proponents rely on the power
of the markets to boost their savings rates.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.900
Assuming you saved your money into a general
stock-market index fund, you might expect

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.470
7-10% rate of return, based on historical
averages.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:16.880
Any experienced investor will tell you that
year-to-year returns will swing wildly, maybe

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:18.030
even crash!

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.390
So that’s where the third rule comes in…

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:26.430
A 1998 study by Trinity University concluded
that a 4% annual withdrawal rate of your money

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:32.220
in retirement should allow you to never out-live
your money - even in a bad economy.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.450
This means that even with the dramatic ups
and downs of the stock and bond market, as

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:42.070
long as your yearly expenses stay below 4%
of your total savings, you should be able

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:45.670
to live off them for… well, theoretically,
forever.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.780
Put another way: you take your annual spending
needs, then multiply it by 25.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.480
That’s the amount you need to become financially
independent.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.480
By now I imagine you’re wondering what it
would take if YOU wanted to to retire early.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.340
I think it’s time to…

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.000
RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.280
Let’s imagine you have a household income
of $85,000, but you live way below your means

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.600
and only need $35,000/yr to be happy.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:19.639
According to our rule of 4%, you’ll need
$875,000 in the bank in order to be financially

00:04:19.639 --> 00:04:20.699
independent.

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:26.759
Through extreme thrift and aggressive cost-cutting,
you’re able to save $50,000/yr, which comes

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:28.919
to 59% of your annual income.

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:33.620
At that rate of savings, and assuming your
stock-index funds got an average return of

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.710
7%, you’ll have hit your goal in...

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.180
12 years.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:43.500
A good income, frugal living, and compound
interest are a powerful wealth-building combination.

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:46.210
You might be wondering “What if I don’t
make a ton of money?

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.520
Is this realistic?”

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.289
A common critique of the Early Retirement
movement is that Adeney and other leaders

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:55.440
of the movement had high-paying jobs in medicine
or engineering.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.440
Making big bucks can certainly speed up the
process.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.920
But it’s not a requirement.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.720
Take Jillian Johnsrud.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.819
She began working towards financial independence
at age 19.

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:10.150
Her husband served in the armed forces and
she worked in customer service and sales.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:16.289
Over the next 13 years they made an average
household income of $60,000, with no year

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:17.949
over six-figures.

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:23.280
And by 32 Jillian had saved enough to be completely
financially independent.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:30.040
All while raising adopted &amp; biological children
and climbing out of $52,000 of debt.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.150
She uses her freed-up time to travel the country,
write, and raise her children.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:38.500
Today she does some work as a writer and coach,
but it’s on her terms.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.460
If you think that “early retirement” is
all about lounging around and avoiding work,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:43.499
you’ve missed the point.

00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:49.349
Instead, it’s about taking an active step
to replace a job you hate with work you love…

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.529
and often finances are the biggest hurdle.

00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:55.789
As Adeney says about the FIRE phenomenon:
“Early retirement means quitting any job

00:05:55.789 --> 00:05:59.759
you wouldn’t do for free – but then
continuing right ahead with work in something

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.480
that works for you, even when you don’t
need the money.”

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.849
And if you’ve already got a fulfilling job
you love-- congratulations, you already have

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:11.460
the benefits of early retirement without having
to save up for it!

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:16.069
So whether or not you want to sprint toward
early retirement, the mindset of reducing

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:21.120
your lifestyle, living simpler, and building
a more rewarding work-life is something we

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.780
should all be aiming for.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.620
And that’s our Two Cents!

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.140
If you were to retire today, what would you do with your newfound freedom?

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.600
Tell us about it in the comments.

